+- **D28 *default* · A −1 that comes too soon after a fast timed delta is a mis-timed message, not saturation.** The
+  decoder flags a timestamp marker (unsigned delta ≥ `TIMER_MARKER_MIN`) as `untrusted` instead (stat
+  `glitchMarkers`, `tools/platter-run`'s "mis-timed markers") when the delta before it was timed, it arrives under
+  half the timestamp's span (2.9 ms) of host time after the last pitch bend, and its ticks at that delta's per-tick
+  interval come to under `MARKER_GLITCH_FRACTION` (new DEFAULTS key, 0.25) of `TIMER_MARKER_MIN`. The estimator
+  then holds the timed ratio, as for the first sample of a host burst (D23), and the next delta is timed from it.
+  Why: three −1s in the census (`captures/2026-10-23-census/08-hand-d1-fast.jsonl` at 4.284 s,
+  `15-motor-d1-33.jsonl` at 5.207 and 37.989 s, all on a backward pass through CC 0 about 1 ms after the message
+  before) read 0.17–0.18 of the limit by that test, and every other −1 after a timed delta in the 23 platter takes
+  reads 0.69 or more (one-off scan over every take on its own deck; `tests/platter-decoder.test.mjs` "M5-07: census
+  08 has 1 …" pins the count); no real interval changes more than 3.53× from one timed delta to the next (§ Platter
+  format). The timestamp after such a −1 carries on from it, so the device lost the message's own interval: read as
+  a marker, it bounded the ratio at 0.17 for one sample and flipped take 15 `backspin` → `scratching` → `backspin`
+  for 1 ms twice. With D28 every estimator update of 15 in 4–63 s is within ±0.01 of −1. The rule looks only at the
+  delta before (a look-ahead would delay every sample by a message) and doesn't require the pass through CC 0 (no
+  forward one was seen in 5,077 passes, but none can be ruled out). Risk: a real saturation can only meet the
+  2.9 ms test in a host-side burst; there the last timed ratio is held until the next sample (up to `STALL_MS` of
+  heartbeats, about 45 ms for a held record) instead of being bounded.
+  To undo: `MARKER_GLITCH_FRACTION` 0 in the config (or drop the `glitch` branch in `createDecoder`). Michael: OK
+  to treat these as glitches? They come only in reverse play here (deck 1's reverse pad on). (2026-10-24)
+
