+**Saturation (spec §5 M5 check 2): saturation confirmed, with one exception.** The question: does an unsigned
+timestamp delta of 16383 (−1) mean "16,383 counts (5.8 ms) or more since the last message"? Host gaps can't settle
+it (the start backlog delivers markers 0 ms apart, and host time jitters by about 1 ms), so the check uses device
+time. A −1 can be tested only when both its neighbours are timed (a valid delta, ticks ≠ 0, host gap ≤ 20 ms, not in
+the backlog). For such a −1, `--markers` prints the neighbours' per-tick interval × the −1's ticks as a fraction of
+16,383, and flags it if that fraction is under 1/4. Between two consecutive timed deltas, the per-tick interval
+changed at most 3.53× in the census (take 29 at 1.362 s; 3.30× in 31, 2.65× in 32, 2.43× in 13; a one-off `node`
+read), so a real ≥ 5.8 ms interval can't sit between neighbours under 1/4 of it. (M5-04 first used the quick
+capture's 1.40×; the review found the census breaks it.)
